WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.987 --> 00:00:05.308
- Any pet owner knows that dogs

00:00:05.308 --> 00:00:07.412
can quickly become an
important part of the family.

00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:09.603
We like to take them along
for the ride whenever we can.

00:00:09.603 --> 00:00:12.187
It's probably okay to leave
them in the car, right?

00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:14.261
Let's ask some people
and see what they think.

00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:15.538
All the puppies!

00:00:15.538 --> 00:00:18.036
Say it's like a 75 degree day.

00:00:18.036 --> 00:00:18.702
- Okay.

00:00:18.702 --> 00:00:20.515
- How long do you think you
can leave your dog in the car?

00:00:20.515 --> 00:00:21.561
- 20 minutes?

00:00:21.561 --> 00:00:23.186
- Two minutes to just run in,

00:00:23.186 --> 00:00:25.233
buy some toothpaste, and get out.

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:26.828
- Maybe five minutes.

00:00:26.828 --> 00:00:28.528
- 10 minutes would even be a long time.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:29.977
- Ideally, I would not
leave a dog in a car

00:00:29.977 --> 00:00:32.313
but I think 10 minutes
with the windows down.

00:00:32.313 --> 00:00:33.561
- 15 minutes tops.

00:00:33.561 --> 00:00:35.130
- I don't know, like an hour?

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.086
- Look at him!

00:00:36.086 --> 00:00:37.451
- So what happens when there's a long line

00:00:37.451 --> 00:00:39.235
or you get distracted inside the store?

00:00:48.015 --> 00:00:49.365
In order to understand this better,

00:00:49.365 --> 00:00:51.643
I decided to sit in my car to
see what it really feels like.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.928
Okay so I'm going to sit
in here for 10 minutes.

00:00:57.928 --> 00:00:59.596
I don't have any windows cracked

00:00:59.596 --> 00:01:01.672
but according to the AMVA,

00:01:01.672 --> 00:01:04.531
cracking the windows actually
makes very little difference.

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:07.853
- Listen, the car is a
perfect storm for pets.

00:01:07.853 --> 00:01:09.754
What we worry about is they're

00:01:09.754 --> 00:01:11.712
not used to a really hot car.

00:01:11.712 --> 00:01:13.410
It's humid in there
because they're panting,

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:17.038
there's no ventilation and a
lot of times, there's no water.

00:01:17.038 --> 00:01:20.159
This is dangerous for pets
and dangerous in general.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:21.305
- According to the thermometer,

00:01:22.655 --> 00:01:24.454
it just hit 90 degrees in here.

00:01:24.454 --> 00:01:26.558
- If you're getting hot, you stay calm.

00:01:26.558 --> 00:01:27.923
That's not how dogs think.

00:01:27.923 --> 00:01:29.634
They don't think, "I'm getting warm,

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:31.884
"I'm getting hot, let me be calm."

00:01:31.884 --> 00:01:33.306
No, they get more anxious.

00:01:33.306 --> 00:01:34.801
- I'm going to take off my jacket

00:01:36.271 --> 00:01:37.631
because it's freaking hot.

00:01:39.361 --> 00:01:40.305
I'm starting to sweat.

00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:42.930
- They do sweat a little
bit between their paws,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.672
a little bit on the top of their nose,

00:01:44.672 --> 00:01:48.372
but that's it and so dogs
mainly cool themselves

00:01:48.372 --> 00:01:50.882
through something called
evaporative cooling.

00:01:50.882 --> 00:01:52.203
That means they pant.

00:01:52.203 --> 00:01:55.047
The harder you pant, the more
worked up you're getting,

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:56.905
and now you're getting even hotter.

00:01:58.315 --> 00:01:59.270
- This is terrible.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.985
- You get a dog who's
panting, who's overheated,

00:02:02.985 --> 00:02:04.684
kidneys can start to shut down,

00:02:04.684 --> 00:02:07.817
we can see direct heat
damage to the brain.

00:02:07.817 --> 00:02:10.792
Multi-organ failure is the
take home message here.

00:02:10.792 --> 00:02:12.665
- I am starting to get like lethargic

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.611
and sleepy and sweaty and really,

00:02:15.611 --> 00:02:16.931
really uncomfortable.

00:02:16.931 --> 00:02:18.194
- And that's why a lot of dogs,

00:02:18.194 --> 00:02:20.211
if you see them, they're getting hot,

00:02:20.211 --> 00:02:22.257
you'll notice them getting more dull,

00:02:22.257 --> 00:02:24.608
they'll seem listless, lethargic,

00:02:24.608 --> 00:02:26.088
just not themselves.

00:02:27.638 --> 00:02:29.672
- This is, I'm get, this
is getting so gross.

00:02:29.672 --> 00:02:32.154
I'm so sweating and this is terrible.

00:02:32.154 --> 00:02:35.404
- I will say this: Never
leave a dog in a hot car,

00:02:35.404 --> 00:02:37.103
never leave the windows up,

00:02:37.103 --> 00:02:39.395
always keep your dog out in an area

00:02:39.395 --> 00:02:40.716
that's fully ventilated.

00:02:40.716 --> 00:02:41.602
- All done?

00:02:41.602 --> 00:02:44.547
10 minutes and the car is
over 95 degrees right now.

00:02:45.449 --> 00:02:46.688
That sucks, that's terrible.

00:02:47.788 --> 00:02:49.238
Ah, the breeze feels so good.

00:02:49.238 --> 00:02:51.779
I'm so happy to be out of that car.

00:02:51.779 --> 00:02:54.478
- If you do have a pet who you
think is having heat stroke,

00:02:54.478 --> 00:02:57.412
there's some keys to preventing

00:02:57.412 --> 00:02:59.021
the worst from happening.

00:02:59.021 --> 00:03:01.620
Get your dog to a vet,
wrap him in cool towels,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.695
blast the AC, and never submerge them.

00:03:04.695 --> 00:03:06.060
- That sucks, that sucks.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.744
It gets really hot and it
sucks even worse for pets

00:03:08.744 --> 00:03:10.022
because they don't know what's happening.

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:12.068
They're just there,
miserable and confused.

00:03:12.068 --> 00:03:14.911
- I'd rather have a vet say
to you, "Everything's fine,

00:03:14.911 --> 00:03:17.320
"just having a little bit of heat stress,"

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.961
which is a mild form of heat stroke,

00:03:18.961 --> 00:03:21.660
than to say, "You waited too late."

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.285
So when in doubt, go to the vet.

00:03:29.481 --> 00:03:31.455
- So what can you do if
you see a dog in danger?

00:03:31.455 --> 00:03:33.181
The Humane Society suggests taking down

00:03:33.181 --> 00:03:35.402
the license plate, model,
and make of the car

00:03:35.402 --> 00:03:37.898
and having local businesses
try to locate the pet owner

00:03:37.898 --> 00:03:40.003
but if that doesn't work,
you can call the local,

00:03:40.003 --> 00:03:42.426
non-emergency police
line or animal control

00:03:42.426 --> 00:03:43.804
and wait for them by the car.

00:03:43.804 --> 00:03:45.477
What do you have to say, Belle?

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:48.626
Great, just chewing the
microphone, perfect.

